  <h2 id="timing">If you are still in school vs already in a commerce degree</h2>

  <p>
    The right first move is different depending on where you actually are.
  </p>

  <ol class="rich-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Class 11-12 Commerce students.</strong>
      {' '}
      Check whether your school offers Informatics Practices and take it seriously if it does — it is the closest thing commerce has to the science-stream Computer Science elective, and most of your classmates will ignore it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Students choosing a degree after 12th.</strong>
      {' '}
      Decide honestly whether the commerce side or the coding side pulls you more, using the 4-Checkpoint Protocol above, before picking between a data-focused BCom, a BBA in Business Analytics, or BCA.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Current BCom, BBA, or CA/CMA/CS students.</strong>
      {' '}
      You do not need to switch degrees. Add SQL, spreadsheet automation, and basic Python alongside your existing coursework, and build one real proof project before you graduate or qualify.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Working commerce graduates.</strong>
      {' '}
      Look for the automation and reporting parts of your current role first. Turning one manual process into a documented, automated one is both a real skill-building exercise and a promotion-worthy result you can point to later.
    </li>
  </ol>

